WEBVTT
TITLE Divide And Conquer — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Section 5 of the GATE 2027 syllabus names divide-and-conquer as one of three design techniques.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Three steps: divide the problem, conquer each part recursively, combine the answers.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Two numbers decide the shape: a, how many parts, and b, how much smaller each part is.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Add the combine cost f(n) and the three numbers give the recurrence; the recurrence gives the time.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Eight elements, split into two halves of four, then two, then one.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Three levels of splitting, and eight one-element lists at the bottom.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Coming back up, seven merges: four of size two, two of size four, one of size eight.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Each level does at most one n of work, and there are three levels: 24 units, which is n log n.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Merging two sorted lists of sizes m and n costs between min(m,n) and m+n-1 comparisons.

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Both bounds are exact: enumerate every interleaving and the extremes are reached.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.000
For eight elements the worst case is seventeen comparisons, and the best is twelve.

12
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
The closed form n·ceil(lg n) - 2^ceil(lg n) + 1 gives 17, and the enumeration agrees for every n from 1 to 8.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
One recurrence for the whole family: T(n) = a·T(n/b) + f(n).

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The tree has log_b n levels and a^(log_b n) leaves, and that leaf count equals n^(log_b a).

15
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Three quantities: the leaves, the work per level, and the height. Whichever dominates sets the answer.

16
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
For merge sort a = 2, b = 2, f(n) = n: height log2 n, leaves n, work n per level.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Case one: the leaves dominate, when f(n) is smaller than n^(log_b a).

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Case two: every level costs the same, when f(n) is exactly n^(log_b a); the answer gains a factor of log n.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Case three: the root dominates, when f(n) is bigger; the answer is f(n).

20
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Checked at n = 256: 2304, 130816 and 33488896, each matched to its closed form.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Quicksort divides around a pivot: n-1 comparisons to partition, then two independent subproblems.

22
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Sorted input with the first element as pivot gives the worst case: 28 comparisons for eight elements.

23
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:54.000
The average is 2(n+1)H_n - 4n: 16.92 for eight elements, which is what the enumeration gives.

24
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Best 13, average 16.92, worst 28 — best and average are both n log n, the worst is not.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Decrease and conquer keeps one subproblem: binary search halves the range and discards the rest.

26
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:16.000
The worst case is floor(lg n) + 1 comparisons: 4 for every n from 8 to 15.

27
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Divide keeps a subproblems; decrease keeps one. That is the gap between n log n and log n.

28
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Quickselect is decrease and conquer with a partition: expected linear, worst quadratic.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Multiplying two n-digit numbers: the schoolbook method does four half-size products.

30
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Karatsuba does three, by adding before multiplying: 3^k products instead of 4^k.

31
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.000
The exponent falls from 2 to log2 3 = 1.585, and 3^k is exactly n^(log2 3).

32
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Counting one unit per multiplication and per addition, Karatsuba wins from n = 4 and loses at n = 2.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
GATE CSE 2019 Q25: the maximum of S(i,j) over fourteen numbers, with divide and conquer allowed. The answer is 29.

34
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:16.000
The page's own divide-and-conquer code returns 29, and brute force over all 105 pairs agrees.

35
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:24.000
GATE CSE 2015 Set 1: the worst-case recurrence for quicksort is T(n) = T(n-1) + O(n), option B.

36
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
GATE CSE 2014 Set 2: T(2^k) = 3T(2^(k-1)) + 1 gives (3^(k+1) - 1)/2, which is Theta(3^k) = Theta(n^(log2 3)).

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Step one: name a, b and f(n), and write the recurrence before solving anything.

38
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Step two: count the levels and the leaves, not the calls you can see in the code.

39
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Step three: compare f(n) with n^(log_b a) to pick the case.

40
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Step four: if the split can be unbalanced, the balanced recurrence is not the answer.

41
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Step five: check the base case and the exact count. An asymptotic answer hides the constant.

